WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:24.960
Blackbeard - Scourge of the Seven Seas

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.939
There are few historical figures whose name
evokes more dread than that of Blackbeard

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:30.939
the pirate.

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:35.340
Undoubtedly the most infamous man to emerge
out of the Golden Age of Piracy of the late

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:42.360
17th-early 18th century, Blackbeard has far
transcended all of his cohorts and has become

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.329
not only an archetype for the villainous pirate
captain, but a pop culture figure who is still

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.550
present today in our movies, TV shows, books,
and video games.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.309
There were other, more successful pirates.

00:00:55.309 --> 00:01:00.360
Bartholomew Roberts aka Black Bart may have
been the most triumphant corsair from the

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.949
Golden Age of Piracy, capturing an estimated
400 ships during his career.

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:10.429
Henry Every, dubbed “The King of Pirates”,
took one massive prize and managed to disappear

00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:14.450
forever, actually getting to enjoy the riches
he plundered.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:19.329
Ching Shih, a female pirate who prowled the
South China Sea, commanded a giant army of

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:21.439
tens of thousands of men.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:27.529
And yet it is Blackbeard that everyone knows
and everyone fears.

00:01:27.529 --> 00:01:32.350
What was it about this larger-than-life figure
that made him stand head-and-shoulders above

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.189
his contemporaries?

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:41.499
Today we will try to find that out as we explore
the life of the dreadful pirate Blackbeard.

00:01:41.499 --> 00:01:48.520
Early Life
Right off the bat, there is a problem with

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.829
trying to tell the story of Blackbeard, one
which is shared with pretty much every other

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:57.389
pirate of his time - a lack of contemporary
sources.

00:01:57.389 --> 00:02:02.100
Blackbeard might be the most famous corsair
in history, but we know shockingly little

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.270
about his life before he raised the black
flag.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:09.149
The main source we have for Blackbeard’s
life and, indeed, for many of his ilk, is

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:14.019
a book originally published in 1724 by one
Captain Charles Johnson.

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:19.420
It went by several very, very long names as
it was reprinted in multiple editions, but

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.270
it is typically known as “A General History
of the Pyrates.”

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:27.599
It is a fascinating read and it certainly
has a lot of juicy stories, but the way it

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:33.060
presents its characters in a somewhat mythical
way makes historians think that it might contain

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.049
its fair share of fabulations, not just facts.

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:39.659
Fittingly, even the author is a mystery to
us.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:45.510
So little is known of Captain Charles Johnson
that people have usually assumed it is a pseudonym.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:50.390
Since 1932 when the idea was first proposed
by writer John Robert Moore, many people have

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:54.969
been convinced that Charles Johnson was an
alias of Daniel Defoe, the English author

00:02:54.969 --> 00:02:57.859
best known for his novel “Robinson Crusoe.”

00:02:57.859 --> 00:03:03.109
Or it is possible that Charles Johnson never
really existed and was simply an amalgam of

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:06.530
anonymous authors who all contributed different
biographies.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.890
Given that Blackbeard was in his late 30s
- early 40s when he died in 1718, he was born

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:15.999
sometime around 1680, presumably in Bristol.

00:03:15.999 --> 00:03:21.500
The name he used most often during his piratical
career was Edward Teach, or sometimes Thatch,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.760
with various spellings of each incarnation.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.989
Truth be told, pirates often used aliases
so we cannot even say with certainty that

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:31.490
either one of these was his real name.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:35.889
It is a bit irrelevant, though, since everyone
came to know him by the sobriquet “Blackbeard.”

00:03:35.889 --> 00:03:41.569
At that time, Bristol was a major international
port, particularly active in the slave trade

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:42.650
across the Atlantic.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:48.209
Therefore, it is almost certain that Teach
started serving aboard a ship from an early

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:53.440
age and some historians believe that he came
from a respectable family and had a good education.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.389
According to Captain Johnson, before turning
to piracy, Teach operated as a privateer out

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:03.480
of Jamaica during the Anglo-French War of
the early 18th century which, itself, was

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.780
part of the larger conflict known as the War
of the Spanish Succession.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.719
Specifically, Teach may have been active in
the American theater known as Queen Anne’s

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:15.930
War where British colonists fought the French
and Spanish.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:21.079
This is bolstered by the fact that, when he
finally captained his own ship, Teach renamed

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:23.240
it to the Queen Anne’s Revenge.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:28.150
In case you ever wondered what the difference
was between a pirate and a privateer, we might

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.669
as well clear it up now.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:35.259
While a pirate was a criminal who operated
mostly on a free-for-all basis and attacked

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:40.580
whatever ship caught his fancy, a privateer
was more like a private contractor under the

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.360
service of his government during wartime.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.830
He received a commission known as a Letter
of Marque and was only allowed to target ships

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:52.550
belonging to the enemy and he had to give
a percentage of the spoils to the government.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.330
The problem was that, once the war was over,
privateers were supposed to stop attacking

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.330
ships.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.270
However, by that time, most of them already
developed a taste for it and they did not

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.970
really have any other marketable skills so
many of them turned to piracy.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:11.419
While we’re at it, let’s cover two more
similar terms - corsair and buccaneer.

00:05:11.419 --> 00:05:16.980
A corsair was an English term used to refer
to foreign privateers and it was intended

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.140
as an insult.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:22.560
Meanwhile, buccaneers were a specific group
of pirates from the Caribbean who started

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.900
out as hunters on islands such as Tortuga
and Hispaniola.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:32.650
They preyed on boar and cattle and sold the
smoked meat to passing ships and, in French,

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.560
the meat was known as viande boucanée.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.180
Because they were tough, skilled hunters,
the buccaneers soon realized that it was easier

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.580
for them to simply attack the ships which
docked on their islands and, soon enough,

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:51.820
they gained a fearsome reputation and were
in demand by all pirate and privateer ships.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:57.379
Raising the Black Flag
The story of Blackbeard starts in earnest

00:05:57.379 --> 00:06:00.330
in 1716 when he took up piracy.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:05.259
You might be surprised to learn that his career
only lasted a little over two years, but it

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:10.289
was enough for his infamy to spread and grow
to the point where he is still a fearsome

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:12.830
character 300 years later.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.550
Teach was somewhat successful as a privateer.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.610
Captain Johnson mentioned that he “often
distinguished himself for his uncommon boldness

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.819
and personal courage”, but specified that
he was never given command of a ship.

00:06:23.819 --> 00:06:28.319
Then, he became faced with the same problem
as all other privateers.

00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:32.650
In 1713, following the signing of the Treaty
of Utrecht, the war ended.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:38.320
Teach no longer had the backing of the British
Government to prey on Spanish and French ships

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.830
so, inevitably, he turned to piracy.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:47.440
Sometime between 1713 and 1716, Edward Teach
left Jamaica for New Providence, an island

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.259
which proved to be an ideal headquarters for
pirates.

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:54.280
Although it once had colonists, it had been
abandoned following numerous Spanish and French

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.919
attacks during the war.

00:06:55.919 --> 00:07:00.389
Even though they were badly damaged, the island
still had multiple structures, including a

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:03.250
fort, so the pirates didn’t have to start
from scratch.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.939
Whilst in New Providence, Teach joined the
crew of another famed pirate of that time,

00:07:07.939 --> 00:07:09.370
Captain Benjamin Hornigold.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:13.360
Hornigold’s notoriety would increase in
just a couple of years when he would accept

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.620
a pardon from the British Government and become
a pirate hunter, tasked with bringing down

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.810
the same men he once sailed with, Blackbeard
included.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.960
For the time being, however, the two had a
strong relationship.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:27.720
Hornigold had clearly been impressed with
Teach’s nautical skills and gave him his

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.129
first command of a ship that they captured.

00:07:30.129 --> 00:07:35.979
It was only a sloop of war, which was a smaller
ship with only two or three masts, but it

00:07:35.979 --> 00:07:37.569
was a start.

00:07:37.569 --> 00:07:45.700
Aboard the Queen Anne’s Revenge
The beginning of 1717 was a fruitful period

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:46.990
for Teach.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:51.349
Alongside Hornigold, he sailed to mainland
America around the coast of Virginia and together

00:07:51.349 --> 00:07:52.639
they took several prizes.

00:07:52.639 --> 00:07:57.080
First, there was a ship coming in from Havana
which had 120 barrels of flour.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:01.229
Then, another sloop from Bermuda which was
carrying wine and, lastly, a ship traveling

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:04.080
from Madeira in Portugal to South Carolina.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.000
After they had enough prowling the waters
off the American coast, the pirates headed

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.990
back to the West Indies in the fall.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:15.030
In November, they captured a French guineaman
called La Concorde which had left Nantes and

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.840
was headed for the island of Martinique.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:22.340
A guineaman was a large cargo ship intended
specifically for the transport of slaves.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:26.599
According to a report given by the ship’s
French Captain Pierre Dosset, La Concorde

00:08:26.599 --> 00:08:33.840
had 16 cannons and 75 crewmen and was carrying
over 500 slaves and around 20 pounds of gold

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:34.840
dust.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:38.420
However, their voyage across the Atlantic
was long and arduous and, by the time they

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:43.200
encountered pirates, they had already lost
16 crewmen and 61 slaves, not to mention the

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.860
fact that another 36 crewmen were stricken
ill with scurvy and dysentery.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:51.810
When they met Teach about 100 miles away from
Martinique, the French had little resistance

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:57.840
to offer, especially as the two pirate ships
had a combined might of 150 men and 20 cannons.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.360
After two volleys fired at La Concorde, Captain
Dosset surrendered his vessel.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.630
Afterwards, the pirates sailed the ships to
the nearby island of Bequia where they dropped

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:08.670
almost everyone off ashore.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:12.820
A few of the French crewmen joined up with
the pirates voluntarily while several useful

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:17.430
members such as surgeons, carpenters, and
a cook were press-ganged into service.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:22.890
Teach preferred this new, larger ship to his
old, tiny sloop so he left that one behind

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.720
with the French sailors and took La Concorde.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:30.140
The French had little say in the matter so
they accepted this new vessel which they renamed

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:33.630
Mauvaise Rencontre (Bad Encounter).

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:38.440
As for La Concorde, with Hornigold’s permission,
Teach captained it and turned it into his

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:41.200
flagship, renaming it the Queen Anne’s Revenge.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.110
We already mentioned that this name came from
Teach serving in Queen Anne’s War, but it

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.870
is also possible that he was a Jacobite, a
person who wanted to restore the House of

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:55.090
Stuart to the throne of Great Britain instead
of the House of Hanover.

00:09:55.090 --> 00:10:01.750
Queen Anne was the last monarch from the House
of Stuart who had passed away in 1714.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:11:02.080
Blackbeard

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:24.430
and Bonnet
Now Teach had a ship that matched his ambitions

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:28.620
and he was ready for the world to get to know
and fear the name “Blackbeard.”

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:32.790
He spent the rest of the year prowling up
and down the islands of the Caribbean, taking

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.580
prizes whenever possible.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:39.420
These included a large vessel called the Great
Allen which the pirates plundered and destroyed,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:44.200
as well as a sloop called the Adventure which
Blackbeard kept and added to his fleet, captained

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.380
by his sailing master, a pirate called Israel
Hands.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:51.670
Near Barbados, the Queen Anne’s Revenge
engaged in a naval battle with a powerful

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:54.220
man-of-war named the HMS Scarborough.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:58.020
Even though the other ship was armed with
30 cannons, the Queen Anne’s Revenge proved

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:01.880
to be more than a match for it and, after
several hours of fighting, the Scarborough

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:04.190
disengaged and retreated to Barbados.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:08.040
Benjamin Hornigold and his ship, the Ranger,
were present for some of these plunders.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:13.630
It is not exactly clear when he and Blackbeard
parted ways, but in January 1718 he traveled

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:17.820
to Jamaica to accept the King’s pardon and
become a pirate hunter.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:21.740
During this time, Blackbeard also made the
acquaintance of another close associate, one

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:27.130
who merits inclusion just because he is unique
in piratical lore - Stede Bonnet.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:30.410
When exactly the two met varies based on the
source.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.570
Some say that Teach and Hornigold first ran
into Bonnet while they were plundering the

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:38.190
coast of America, while Captain Johnson specified
that Blackbeard met the other pirate captain

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:40.630
after acquiring the Queen Anne’s Revenge.

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:45.910
What made Stede Bonnet truly special was that
he seemingly had it all, yet gave it away

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:47.040
to become a pirate.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.540
He was a wealthy, respectable landowner with
a sugar plantation in Barbados.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:56.920
Even so, he borrowed money to buy and outfit
a ship and secure a crew and left his past

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:59.280
life behind and raised the black flag.

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.180
We can only speculate as to his motivation.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:06.191
Maybe he went through an extreme “midlife
crisis” or maybe he was just really, really

00:13:06.191 --> 00:13:09.180
bored and wanted some adventure?

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:13.240
Contemporary accounts suggested that Bonnet
suffered “some discomforts he found in the

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:14.240
married state”.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:19.060
However, becoming a pirate just to get away
from a nagging wife seems a tad extreme to

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:23.550
historians, who argue that Bonnet may have
been mentally unstable.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.910
Whatever the reason, Stede Bonnet became a
pirate captain even though he had no knowledge

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:27.910
of sailing.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:31.850
Teach picked up on this pretty quick when
the two met and used it to his advantage in

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.240
order to add another ship under his command.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:38.050
Bonnet’s men were perfectly happy serving
under the mighty Blackbeard instead of an

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.540
inexperienced captain.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.300
Bonnet himself was recovering from an injury
and was made a permanent guest aboard the

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.750
Queen Anne’s Revenge while one of Teach’s
men, a first mate named Richards, captained

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:49.090
Bonnet’s ship, the Revenge.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:54.180
Of course, in this case, “permanent guest”
was a polite way of saying prisoner as it

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.580
is hard to know what would have happened if
Bonnet tried to forcefully regain command

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:58.600
of his ship.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:02.900
This was never the case, however, as the “Gentleman
Pirate”, as he was sometimes called, seemed

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:07.180
content sailing alongside Blackbeard and just
being there for the ride.

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:11.630
Eventually, Blackbeard fully betrayed Bonnet
when the captain decided he had too many ships

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:12.630
in his fleet.

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:17.340
He stripped the Revenge of loot and supplies
and beached it, also marooning most of its

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:18.340
crew on a sandbar.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:22.150
To his credit, Bonnet did learn a thing or
two about being a pirate while sailing with

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:23.150
Teach.

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:27.750
He was able to get the Revenge seaworthy again,
secured a crew and went in pursuit of his

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:29.110
treacherous former companion.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:33.790
It would have been interesting to get a final
confrontation between these two and see who

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.000
came out on top.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.510
Alas, Bonnet never met Blackbeard again.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:42.940
Instead, he was caught and arrested and, after
numerous delays, hanged for piracy.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:47.850
On the bright side (for us, at least), Bonnet’s
trial transcript became an important source

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:56.180
for a big chunk of Blackbeard’s career.

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:58.850
The Cult of Blackbeard
The first half of 1718 saw Blackbeard at the

00:14:58.850 --> 00:14:59.850
height of his power.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:04.560
He took prize after prize with little to no
opposition because his fleet was mighty enough

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:09.740
that no single ship stood a chance against
them, but also because people began fearing

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:10.940
the name “Blackbeard.”

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:15.820
If Teach was good at anything, it was cultivating
a menacing reputation for himself which sent

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:20.940
chills down everyone’s spine and made ship
captains surrender before there was any need

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.290
for actual combat.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:24.470
Physically, Teach was an imposing man.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:29.650
He was described as being tall with wide shoulders
and, obviously, he had a very long, dark beard.

00:15:29.650 --> 00:15:34.230
He wore a sling over both shoulders which
contained braces for six pistols that he held

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:36.000
across his chest like bandoliers.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:41.900
Allegedly, he was in the habit of taking long,
slow-burning fuses, lighting them and tucking

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:46.810
them under his hat so that little wisps of
smoke would form around his head, giving him

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.190
an otherworldly, frightening appearance.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:53.530
Curiously, Teach also apparently liked to
take his long beard and braid it into pigtails

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:55.400
tied off with colored ribbons.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:16:00.390
Doesn’t exactly sound particularly terrifying,
but I guess people were a lot easier to scare

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:01.390
300 years ago.

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:05.660
Whatever he did, it worked, and most ships
were ready to surrender as soon as they saw

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:07.320
Blackbeard’s flag.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:11.220
Speaking of which...what was Blackbeard’s
flag, exactly?

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:13.400
That’s a hard question to answer.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:17.210
There is one design which had been associated
with the pirate for a long time.

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:22.200
It features a horned skeleton holding an hourglass
in one hand and a spear in the other which

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:23.240
is piercing a heart.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:28.030
It is an interesting design, but it appears
to only be a hundred years old as it was actually

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:33.150
first described in a magazine in 1912 with
no evidence to suggest it was ever used by

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:34.150
Blackbeard.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.170
As far as contemporary accounts are concerned,
there is a single description which appeared

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:42.790
in a newspaper, detailing an attack by Blackbeard’s
fleet on a merchant ship.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:47.110
It mentions that there were five pirate vessels
in total - two of them had black flags with

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:51.430
death’s heads on them while the other three
had bloody flags.

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:55.450
Going by this information, it is possible
that Blackbeard used the stereotypical skull

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:58.540
&amp; crossbones for his design.

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:06.730
The Blockade of Charles Town
In the spring of 1718, Blackbeard had enough

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.039
of the Caribbean and sailed back to America.

00:17:09.039 --> 00:17:13.779
Here, besides taking a few more prizes whenever
the occasion presented itself, Teach embarked

00:17:13.779 --> 00:17:18.460
on his most brazen endeavor - blockading the
city of Charles Town, South Carolina, today

00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:19.770
called Charleston.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:24.579
The pirates boarded and seized all ships that
tried to enter or leave the port.

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:29.179
One of those vessels, the Crowley, contained
a lot of rich, prominent Charles Town citizens

00:17:29.179 --> 00:17:30.200
headed for London.

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:34.220
Blackbeard took them as hostages and told
the governor that, if he didn’t meet his

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:39.230
demands, he would send the governor all their
heads and burn down the ships he had seized.

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:44.860
Blackbeard’s ransom demand was rather dull,
but practical - he wanted a chest full of

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:45.860
medicine.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:49.409
He sent one of the hostages, Mr. Marks, and
a few of his own men ashore and gave them

00:17:49.409 --> 00:17:52.850
two days to return before he started chopping
off heads.

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:57.610
Those days passed without response but, fortunately
for everyone involved, the captain wasn’t

00:17:57.610 --> 00:17:59.240
a premature decapitator.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:03.919
Blackbeard delayed his punishment long enough
that Marks arrived with the medicine and explained

00:18:03.919 --> 00:18:08.250
that the reason why it took longer than expected
was because the pirates got drunk in the city

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:09.460
and couldn’t be found.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:13.549
Blackbeard was content with this explanation
and released all his hostages safely...without

00:18:13.549 --> 00:18:16.379
their valuables, of course.

00:18:16.379 --> 00:18:20.970
From there, the pirate fleet moved on to North
Carolina where something bizarre happened

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:24.809
at Beaufort Inlet - Blackbeard lost the Queen
Anne’s Revenge.

00:18:24.809 --> 00:18:27.370
Apparently, the intention was to careen it.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:31.201
Careening is the practice of grounding a ship
intentionally at high tide so that, during

00:18:31.201 --> 00:18:35.560
low tide, you can repair or clean the bottom
of the hull that’s normally underwater.

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:39.870
However, the ship apparently went too far
ashore and ran aground into a sandbar.

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:44.200
They tried to use one of the sloops to tow
the ship back to water, but that one ran aground,

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:47.870
as well, and both sustained too much damage
to be usable again.

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:51.200
Just like that, Blackbeard was left only with
two sloops.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:55.620
This proved to be a real boon for modern archaeologists
as a search team found the wreckage of the

00:18:55.620 --> 00:19:00.840
Queen Anne’s Revenge in 1996 and researchers
have been studying and excavating it for over

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:01.879
two decades.

00:19:01.879 --> 00:19:07.540
It wasn’t until 2011 that it was conclusively
identified as the flagship of Blackbeard the

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:10.570
pirate, but it did raise a question regarding
how it got there.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:15.750
Losing two ships while careening would have
been, even using the gentlest terms, a foolish

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:16.750
mistake.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.610
Up until that point, Blackbeard had shown
himself a capable captain.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:24.559
That is why some historians speculate that
he may have done it on purpose to break up

00:19:24.559 --> 00:19:26.450
his crew.

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:33.789
The Pardon &amp; Passing of Blackbeard
Next up, Teach went to Bath, North Carolina,

00:19:33.789 --> 00:19:37.879
and accepted the King’s pardon like Hornigold
and many other comrades-in-arms.

00:19:37.879 --> 00:19:43.610
In an effort to curb piracy, King George I
extended a one-time offer of pardon to all

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:48.289
pirates who surrendered willfully, with the
promise that those who didn’t would be hunted

00:19:48.289 --> 00:19:50.230
down without mercy.

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:55.009
Whether Blackbeard did this because he lost
his flagship or whether he ran the ship aground

00:19:55.009 --> 00:19:58.549
because this was his plan all along, we cannot
say.

00:19:58.549 --> 00:20:03.259
What we do know is that he became close friends
with the North Carolina Governor Charles Eden

00:20:03.259 --> 00:20:09.200
who not only gave Teach his pardon, but also
a commission as a privateer aboard his remaining

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:10.370
sloop, the Adventure.

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:14.950
The pirate, more or less, continued his plundering
ways, but now he had the approval of the governor

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.759
who, most likely, received a cut of his spoils.

00:20:17.759 --> 00:20:22.710
Eden may have been happy with Blackbeard prowling
his waters, but the nearby Governor of Virginia

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.230
Alexander Spotswood was not.

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:28.799
Some of the captain’s former crew members
had already begun causing troubles in his

00:20:28.799 --> 00:20:33.620
territory and having Blackbeard in the area
severely put a hamper on local trade.

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:38.610
He had little legal recourse against the pirate,
but he didn’t care - he wanted Blackbeard

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:40.659
gone one way or another.

00:20:40.659 --> 00:20:44.779
Technically, Spotswood commissioned two armed
sloops to go capture Teach for several acts

00:20:44.779 --> 00:20:49.279
of piracy which he may have committed after
the cut-off date for the pardon which was

00:20:49.279 --> 00:20:50.419
January 5.

00:20:50.419 --> 00:20:55.840
However, he did so knowing full well that
this would most likely lead to a battle.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:21:00.899
The Virginia force was led by Lieutenant Robert
Maynard, a man described by Captain Johnson

00:21:00.899 --> 00:21:04.419
as “a gentleman of great bravery and resolution.”

00:21:04.419 --> 00:21:09.159
On the morning of November 22, 1718, the two
sloops approached the Adventure in the channel

00:21:09.159 --> 00:21:13.139
leading to Ocracoke Island and engaged it
in a fight.

00:21:13.139 --> 00:21:17.559
First came several volleys of cannon fire
which proved quite destructive to Maynard’s

00:21:17.559 --> 00:21:19.779
forces and gave Blackbeard the advantage.

00:21:19.779 --> 00:21:24.419
This prompted the pirate to board the lieutenant’s
ship but, unbeknownst to him, he walked into

00:21:24.419 --> 00:21:25.420
an ambush.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:29.769
Maynard had ordered most of his men to hide
below deck and trick the pirates into thinking

00:21:29.769 --> 00:21:30.879
they were severely outnumbered.

00:21:30.879 --> 00:21:35.169
As soon as Blackbeard and his cohorts were
aboard, the British sailors pounced.

00:21:35.169 --> 00:21:38.159
According to Maynard’s account, the fight
lasted six minutes.

00:21:38.159 --> 00:21:42.950
Afterwards, the infamous Blackbeard lay dead
after being shot five times and cut twenty

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:43.950
times.

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:45.730
The surviving pirates quickly gave up.

00:21:45.730 --> 00:21:50.370
Maynard had Blackbeard’s head tied to his
ship’s bowsprit while the rest of his remains

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:51.960
were thrown in the water.

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:55.009
That was the end of the world’s most notorious
pirate.

00:21:55.009 --> 00:21:59.509
It was somewhat fitting that Blackbeard died
in battle and got sent to Davy Jones’ locker

00:21:59.509 --> 00:22:02.620
instead of being hanged like most of his contemporaries.

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:07.490
If anything, this violent end only helped
increase the legend of Blackbeard, as evidenced

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:28.190
by the fact that it is still going strong
today.

